.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH qsh-pair-ssh 1  "ssh 0.5.6" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-pair\-ssh \- Pin this machine and a remote one to each other over ssh, then check the pair with a QUIC handshake (ADR\-0044, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh pair ssh\fR [\fB\-\-as\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\-as\fR] [\fB\-\-role\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-\-address\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-reverse\fR] [\fB\-\-to\fR] [\fB\-\-remote\-qsh\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fI[USER@]HOST\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pin this machine and a remote one to each other over ssh, then check the pair with a QUIC handshake (ADR\-0044, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11).
.PP
Runs the system `ssh` to `DEST` (anything `ssh` accepts, so `~/.ssh/config` aliases, keys, agents and 2FA prompts apply) and has the remote `qsh` pin this machine\*(Aqs public fingerprint; this machine then pins the remote\*(Aqs, with trust role `none`. ssh authenticates the exchange: no key, passphrase or agent socket is ever read by qsh. It installs and starts nothing on the remote and writes no `hosts.toml`, `config.toml` or `acl.toml`.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Name this machine pins the remote under, instead of the host part of `DEST`
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\-as\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Name the remote pins this machine under (default: the first label of this machine\*(Aqs host name, then its device id)
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fI<ROLE>\fR
Trust role of the pin written on the remote: one of `full`, `exec`, `tunnel`, `read\-only`, `reverse`, `none` (default `full`, which is what the ssh login on that account could do anyway, ADR\-0044 decision 8). An existing remote pin keeps its role. This machine\*(Aqs own pin is always `none`
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-\-address\fR \fI<HOST[:PORT]>\fR
`host[:port]` this machine records for the remote, instead of the ssh `HostName` plus the port the remote `qsh serve` reports. With no `:port`, port 4433 is assumed
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-reverse\fR
Reverse direction: the remote dials this machine\*(Aqs `qsh listen` instead of this machine dialing the remote. Goes with `\-\-to`, and does not combine with `\-\-address` (either mistake is an `INVALID_ARGUMENT` before ssh runs). The remote pins this machine at the `\-\-to` address with the `\-\-role` role; this machine pins the remote without an address and with role `reverse`. Nothing is started on the remote and no connection is tried: the result names the `qsh serve \-\-to` command to run there
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fI<HOST[:PORT]>\fR
With `\-\-reverse`: the `host[:port]` the remote dials to reach this machine\*(Aqs `qsh listen`. With no `:port`, port 4433 is assumed
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\-qsh\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
The `qsh` to run on the remote (default: `[ssh].remote_qsh` in `config.toml`, then `qsh`)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fI[USER@]HOST\fR>
`[user@]host`, exactly as `ssh` takes it: a `Host` alias from `~/.ssh/config` works. The host part is also the default name this machine pins the remote under; it must not start with `\-`
.SH VERSION
v0.5.6
